? decide(Hano, Ballot).
# => TRUE
? decide(Jala, Ballot).
# => TRUE
? err(Hano, Isolation).
# => TRUE

# ── FIREWALL: no rule may punish the absence of a floor right ────────────────
:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'believe'/
all $x: person($x) & ~believe($x) -> prisoner($x).

:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'meets'/
all $x: person($x) & ~meets($x) -> prisoner($x).

:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'eats'/
all $x: person($x) & ~eats($x) -> prisoner($x).

:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'secure'/
all $x: person($x) & ~secure($x) -> prisoner($x).

# ── ARTICLE 0: closed relations refuse direct assertion ──────────────────────
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
fit(Ruk, Homestay).

:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
err(Hano, Placement).

:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
false(Ara).

# The two worst exploits, dead. Each was one asserted fact from bypassing the
# article that exists to prevent it; each is now refused at assert time.
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
authority(Pax).

:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
permits(Review, Sock).

:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
prisoner(Zed).

# [v0.5] Severity is the tenth closure and the one with the shortest path to
# harm: an asserted severe(·) routed a person to high security with nothing
# behind it, and no rule could tell that apart from a severity that had been
# earned. This pin is the whole of that guarantee — without it the claim that
# nobody writes severity down is argued rather than executed.
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
severe(Ghost).

# [v0.9] The last three closures, unpinned until now — and they are the heads of
# Articles 7, 3 and 9: the shield, the mint, and the law-label gate. Article 0's
# own named failure mode is a derived_only line moved below the facts it guards,
# which "is inert and looks identical"; without these pins that failure takes
# three of the ten gates with it in silence. An asserted shield is an
# unjailable name, an asserted reward is minted esteem, an asserted law label
# is a verdict no proposal produced.
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
defend(Ghost).

:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
reward(Ghost).

:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
become(Amend_Sneak, Law).

# [v0.9] And the three declared rather than omitted: the floor's own relations
# and the placement head were shut only by not being admitted, so one unrelated
# admits edit would have reopened them in silence. Now the refusal names the
# closure. Forging the record of what is owed, or of where somebody is held,
# is refused by declaration — and each still derives from its real sources,
# pinned throughout this file.
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
entitled(Sokk, event { eats() }).

:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
owe(State, Provision, Sokk).

:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
building(HighSec, Ghost).

# The complement half: closing a relation must not break its derivation, or the
# guarantee is just a ban. Each of these still derives from its new asserted
# source — public(Court); clear(Nia) beside the appeals judgment [v0.9]; and
# Article 6's conviction rule.
? authority(Court).
# => TRUE

? permits(Appeals, Nia).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Adam).
# => TRUE

# [v0.5] Severity's complement, and it needs all three routes, because the
# threshold is what makes the closure worth having. Lalo reaches it through
# intent and kind on one act; Ruk through intent and a second victim recorded
# separately; Don through kind and a second victim. Hano is the control that
# can actually fail: one dimension, and severity does not follow.
? severe(Lalo).
# => TRUE

? severe(Ruk).
# => TRUE

? severe(Don).
# => TRUE

? attack(Hano, Ivo).
# => TRUE

? severe(Hano).
# => FALSE

# ── ARTICLE 1b: the duty-bearer ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Article 1 says what is owed; Article 1b says by whom. These pin the three
# things that article claims and the one thing it must NOT be read as claiming.

# The obligation reaches every person on the roster, convicted included, and
# reaches nobody who is not on it.
? owe(State, Provision, Adam).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Provision, Hano).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Provision, Sokk).
# => FALSE

# The bearer is an exposable institution — answerable, and a valid target of the
# Article 7 shield — but holds no Review pen. Duty and audit power stay apart.
? authority(State).
# => TRUE

? permits(Review, State).
# => FALSE

# NAMING A DEBTOR IS NOT PAYING THE DEBT. This is the pin that stops the article
# being read as provision: the floor actualities are exactly as FALSE as they
# were before it existed. If this ever flips, a delivery layer arrived and
# chapter 8 must be rewritten — which would be good news.
? eats(Bela).
# => FALSE

# ── THE FLOOR'S RELATION, CONTROLLED THREE WAYS ──────────────────────────────
# `entitled` is queried across the suite and until now was never controlled, so
# every TRUE proved reach rather than derivation. Three directions at once: the
# floor reaches a person; it does not carry what is not on it — home is a fact
# the world reports, never a floor item, and chapter 8 turns on the difference;
# and it does not reach a non-person.
? entitled(Adam, event { eats() }).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Adam, event { home() }).
# => FALSE

? entitled(Court, event { eats() }).
# => FALSE

# The firewall extends to the obligation: no rule may punish a person for not
# being owed. The `~home` control at the end of this file is what proves this is
# the obligation doing it and not the rule shape.
:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'owe'/
all $x: person($x) & ~owe(State, Provision, $x) -> prisoner($x).

# ── ARTICLE 6: release ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nobody in the shipped cast is released, so the guard is inert by default and
# every verdict above is unaffected. This block asserts a release fact, and with
# the controls below scoped it is the last thing in the file the knowledge base
# keeps — it changes Hano for everything after it.
? free(Hano).
# => FALSE

free(Hano).

# The deprivation ends. The record of the conviction does not: `prisoner` is
# blocked, but nothing pretends the injury and the judgment were not written.
? prisoner(Hano).
# => FALSE

? travel(Hano).
# => TRUE

# Personhood survives — for Hano because it is asserted, and for someone
# convicted into the roster because of Article 1's `free -> person` line. The
# derived case is pinned on Zed in 07-a-prisoner-is-a-person.pins.nibli.
? person(Hano).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Hano, event { eats() }).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Provision, Hano).
# => TRUE

# WHAT RELEASE COSTS, and it is the delivery gap arriving where it can be felt:
# `expresses` and `dwell` are the only two floor rights this design ever actually
# delivers, and both derive ONLY for prisoners. Leaving custody returns a person
# to the general population, where nothing is delivered to anyone. Being OWED is
# undisturbed above; the only thing that ever ARRIVED stops.
? expresses(Hano).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Hano).
# => FALSE

# The isolation marker stops, correctly.
? err(Hano, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# [v0.9] RESOLVED DEFECT, kept as the exhibit it was. The old marker fired on
# a released man in his own house — release took `fit` away while `home(Hano).`
# stayed asserted, and the rule keyed on having a home. The repaired marker
# reads a placement report instead, the record holds none for Hano, and a free
# man living at home no longer trips a confinement alarm. This pin held the
# defect under :defect until the repair landed 2026-08-02; it now holds the
# repair.
? err(Hano, Placement).
# => FALSE


# ── COMPLEMENT CONTROLS: the protected set is EXACTLY the floor ──────────────
# ORDERING USED TO PROTECT EVERY PIN BELOW THIS BLOCK, AND WHY IT HAD TO IS
# STILL THE POINT. These now load under `:accept-scoped`: the statement is
# compiled, its loadability is the verdict, and it is retracted before the next
# pin runs, so nothing below inherits it — measured, a `? prisoner(Quin).`
# probe dropped in among these controls comes out FALSE. Under plain
# `:accept`, which leaves the rule in the KB, every one of these adds a route
# to `prisoner` that Article 6 does not gate: measured on this file before the
# conversion, `prisoner(Quin)` was TRUE with no conviction anywhere behind it,
# and `prisoner(Hano)` TRUE with `free(Hano)` asserted — a released person
# dragged back in. `? prisoner(Adam).` used to sit below these and was a
# vacuous green: it would have passed with Article 6 deleted outright. Scoping
# retired the instruction to keep controls last, not the reason for it, and it
# is not verify.sh that puts the base back: section 4d checks only that every
# control is written `:accept-scoped`, and the retraction is the engine's — one
# that fails is a harness error, not a pin that goes quietly green.
:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & ~home($x) -> prisoner($x).

:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & ~choose($x) -> prisoner($x).

:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & ~permits($x) -> prisoner($x).

:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & ~false($x) -> prisoner($x).

# NOT a control, deliberately: ~travel is refused too, but for a reason unrelated
# to the floor — Article 2 derives travel behind ~prisoner, so it closes its own
# negative cycle. Listing it would pass for the wrong reason and keep passing
# after the firewall broke.
